{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/gender-role",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "gender role"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "The learned socially constructed definitions, expectations, and attitudes socially assigned to individuals based on their perceived gender within cis-heteronormative systems."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "These culturally constructed codes of behaviour dictate how society expects people to dress, speak, act, work, and even love. Gendered roles can perpetuate gender stereotypes, for instance, promoting models of toxic masculinity and submissive femininity. They likewise perpetuate the gender binary male/female silencing non-gender conforming identifications. Gender division not only establishes differences between women and men but also reinforces and perpetuates relationships of inequality. Social learning plays a fundamental role in shaping the expectations, attitudes, and behaviours associated with gender. Through various agents of socialisation — including the family, educational institutions, the media, and peer groups — different activities, responsibilities, and social roles are assigned to men and women. It should be noted that gendered roles interlock with other social ideologies, such as racial biases."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "7.4.3"
  ],
